00:00:30.907 --> 00:00:34.640
The ciliates we’re going to talk about today
are kind of frustrating.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.100
At this point in our journey, we’ve gotten
used to the fact that the microcosmos is an

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.190
indecipherable mess at times, filled with
organisms that look like each other, and who

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:49.970
have familial relationships that seem obvious
but then turn out to be a figment of our own

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.180
limited imaginations.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:57.860
And these ciliates are yet another entry in
the long-standing saga of ever-changing taxonomies

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.190
that define our understanding of microbial
species.

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:05.304
The plot twist is inevitable.

00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:11.514
We are going to be showing you these ciliates
and talking about them like they are related,

00:01:11.514 --> 00:01:16.030
only to reveal at the end that they are not
nearly as closely related as microbiologists

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.030
once thought.

00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:22.670
But we still need to talk about these ciliates
because, they are inescapable.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.640
They are always around, showing up in so many
of our samples.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.644
And we also need to talk about these ciliates
because no matter how frustrating they are,

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:33.813
they’re also a ton of fun to watch.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.701
These are a subclass of ciliates called hypotrichs.

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:45.780
You might recognize that name because it’s
shown up as a label on our channel before.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.359
And perhaps you’ve wondered what exactly
that means.

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:52.959
“Hypotrich” sounds very cool, but it’s
not really a super helpful label if you don’t

00:01:52.959 --> 00:01:54.670
know what a hypotrich actually is.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.569
And that is why we’re here today.

00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:00.349
But just so you know, it’s not a very specific
label.

00:02:00.349 --> 00:02:04.999
It does not tell us a whole lot about what
species of organism we’re looking at.

00:02:04.999 --> 00:02:07.200
But sometimes, it’s the best we can do.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.570
After all, there are countless species of
microbes in the world, many of which have

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:17.050
names buried in books and papers we haven’t
come across yet, and many others of which

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.790
literally don't have names yet.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:25.720
So for James, our master of microscopes, and—it
should be said—a connoisseur of ciliates,

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:31.200
labeling microbes sometimes becomes a matter
of figuring out just how specific he can get.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.590
Sometimes, the best he can do is something
broad, calling it something like “ciliate.”

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.220
But sometimes there are details that help
him narrow in a little closer.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.743
And hypotrichs have some pretty helpful details.

00:02:45.274 --> 00:02:49.205
For one, they usually have a big mouth and
a flat body.

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:54.832
Some even have interesting pigments, like
the gold dotting this frantically spiraling individual.

00:02:54.832 --> 00:02:56.272
Or this pink-tinted one.

00:02:56.714 --> 00:03:01.870
Now we’re not sure what those pigments are or
what purpose they serve, though based on what

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:07.187
we’ve seen in other organisms, they could
be a way to block harmful UV light from the sun.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:12.260
But things like a flat body or big mouth or
pigments are not enough.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.633
What really sets hypotrichs apart from other
ciliates is their thick hair.

00:03:20.561 --> 00:03:27.095
Ciliates are hairy single-celled creatures,
surrounded by thin fibrous organelles called cilia.

00:03:27.095 --> 00:03:32.730
And the arrangement of these cilia do so much
to shape the way these organisms live, from

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:37.730
the way the organisms move around the microcosmos
to the food the cilia draw in towards the

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:38.813
organism’s mouth.

00:03:38.813 --> 00:03:43.330
But even among ciliates, hypotrichs stand
out for how they use their cilia.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:47.100
They are not content to just have the thin
structures their compatriots do.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:52.560
No, hypotrichs take these individual cilia
and bundle them together into something bigger

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.420
called a cirri, like a rope made of smaller
strands.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:02.839
In some species, they may even have more than
100 cilia fused together in their cirri.

00:04:02.839 --> 00:04:08.583
And it’s thanks to these cirri that hypotrichs
do something unusual for single-celled organisms.

00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:11.400
They walk.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.520
You can really see it with this Aspidisca,
which looks like it’s crawling across the

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.819
screen like a little cartoon bug.

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:22.279
So the cirri help us know that we are looking
at a hypotrich ciliate.

00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:27.030
But to narrow down the genus or species, we
need to be able to see all of the cirri

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:30.917
so we can make out the patterns they form
along the hypotrich’s body.

00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:35.811
And it can be tough to see all of those cirri,
which is often why we cannot identify our hypotrichs.

00:04:35.811 --> 00:04:39.020
But there are some genuses that make it easier
on us.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.889
For example, this stichotrich has a really
distinct look to it thanks to its shape and

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:45.569
the spiral of cirri traveling down it.

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:49.930
And then there’s this diophrys, which has…well,
it has a hairy butt.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:51.889
And that makes it pretty easy to identify.

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:56.259
It’s also very fast, we actually had to
record this in slow motion to be able to show

00:04:56.259 --> 00:04:57.439
it to you.

00:04:57.439 --> 00:05:00.673
But this is also where things get confusing
and complicated.

00:05:00.673 --> 00:05:04.000
It’s where that plot twist comes in, the
one we told you about in the beginning so

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.300
it’s not really a plot twist anymore.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.039
It’s like watching The Empire Strikes Back
for the fiftieth time when you know what’s

00:05:09.039 --> 00:05:13.990
coming, though I guess in this version, family
bonds are getting broken instead of revealed.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.349
Because we’re talking about all of these
organisms like they’re hypotrichs, except

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:21.090
that it’s not clear whether that’s actually
what they are.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:26.409
And when we’ve been diving into the literature,
the classifications get kind of confusing.

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:30.210
What makes us feel a little better about ourselves
is that it seems like the biologists find

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.230
it a bit unfathomable as well.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:39.863
The 1979 edition of The Ciliated Protozoa
described the taxonomy of hypotrichs as “aggravating.”

00:05:39.863 --> 00:05:42.474
It's good when scientists just tell it like it is.

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:48.539
Decades later, in 2021, a paper described
them as “one of the most confused groups

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:51.620
in terms of their systematics,” .

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.870
It turns out that what defines an organism
as a hypotrich or even a particular subclass

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.930
of hypotrich depends so much on what you
are reading.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.509
One of the more recent works dedicated to
bringing more order to ciliate classification

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:08.935
found that there were at least ten different

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:12.966
taxonomic systems devised 
around categorizing hypotrichs.

00:06:12.966 --> 00:06:17.870
And that’s how you get a story like the
tale of the Euplotes.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:24.400
These ciliates were first described in 1733,
and about 100 years later, the German microbiologist

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.220
Christian Ehrenberg created the genus Euplotes
around them.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.939
And they would end up being lumped in with
many of the other hypotrichs as their own

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:37.069
subclass, becoming one of the old guards of
the hypotrichs.

00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:43.910
Except, eventually, for taxonomic reasons,
the Euplotes eventually became their own thing,

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.779
separate from the hypotrichs but still related.

00:06:46.779 --> 00:06:51.900
The hypotrichs and euplotes simply became
relatives, grouped together under a different

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.739
family name: the spirotrichs.

00:06:54.739 --> 00:06:59.659
And on the one hand, this is the kind of thing
that makes hypotrichs—and I guess, spirotrichs as well—

00:06:59.659 --> 00:07:01.289
so frustrating to talk about.

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:03.689
But it’s also something very cool.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:08.759
It’s like the microcosmos is a library that is
constantly acquiring new books.

00:07:08.759 --> 00:07:13.229
And the more we read those books and add new
ones to our shelves, the more we find ourselves

00:07:13.229 --> 00:07:20.615
needing to rearrange them so their location
and neighbors reflect all that we have discovered.

00:07:26.965 --> 00:07:31.717
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

